To terminate a C program, you can use the `exit()` function or the `return` statement.

The `exit()` function is used to exit the program immediately and return control to the operating system. It takes an integer argument that specifies the status code of the termination:

```c
int main() {
    // some code here
    exit(0);  // normal termination with status code 0
}
```

If you want to terminate the program without returning any value (i.e., without calling any functions), you can use the `return` statement:

```c
int main() {
    // some code here
    return;  // terminates the program without returning anything
}
```

In both cases, after the program has terminated, it will be returned to the operating system where it was originally called from.
